From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 01:40:58 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] v4l2: keep an encoder's level to what its client asked for

Two things made an encoder state a level other than the one its client
asked for.

The H.264 profile and level setter remembered the lowest level any client
had asked for in a static shared by every encoder in the process, and
applied it to the next client that asked for a higher one. The service runs
every encoder of every app in one process, so an app that recorded at level
1.3 capped the next stream at 1.3: a stream that needed 2.0 came out as
1.3 whenever 1.3 had been configured earlier. The record goes; the HEVC
setter does not have one either.

The setter also raised the requested level to the lowest one that held the
picture, the frame rate and the bit rate it saw. The framework sets those
parameters in more than one step, so a setter that ran while the frame rate
was still its default of 30 left a client that asked for level 1 at 15
frames per second with level 1.1. The setter now keeps the client's level,
replacing only one the interface does not offer. getEffectiveLevel() works
the level out from the final parameters when the device is configured, and
the component uses it for the level control and for the frame rate limit.

A variable bit rate stream peaks at twice its target, which can pass the
bit rate its level allows and make the device state a higher level than the
client asked for. getPeakBitrate() keeps the peak within the level's limit
and never below the target.
